                "Pulling it at 160°F or higher: pork tenderloin is safe and best at 145°F; anything past that turns dry and stringy since there's almost no fat to buffer overcooking.",
                "Slicing right out of the oven: the juices haven't redistributed yet and will pool out onto the cutting board instead of staying in the meat.",
                "Using a cold skillet for the sear: a pan that isn't fully preheated steams the surface instead of browning it, leaving a pale, flabby crust.",
