    {
        "slug": "caribbean-food",
        "template_type": "ingredient_hub",
        "title": "Caribbean Food",
        "batch_number": 2,
        "content": {
            "meta_description": "What defines Caribbean cooking, substitutes for hard-to-find ingredients like scotch bonnet and culantro, how to store key staples, and how the cuisine differs island to island.",
            "hero_image_query": "caribbean food spread jerk chicken rice and peas plantains",
            "image_alt": "A table spread of Caribbean dishes including jerk chicken, rice and peas, fried plantains, and a bowl of callaloo stew.",
            "description": "Caribbean food isn't one cuisine but a family of related cooking traditions shaped by West African, Indigenous Taino, Indian, Chinese, and European influences layered over centuries of colonization and migration. Across Jamaica, Trinidad and Tobago, Haiti, the Dominican Republic, Puerto Rico, and the smaller islands, a few threads run through nearly all of it: scotch bonnet or habanero chiles for heat, allspice and thyme for a distinct savory backbone, coconut milk for richness, starchy staples like rice, plantain, cassava, and yam, and slow-cooked stews built on a sofrito or seasoning base browned in oil.",
            "substitutes": [
                {
                    "name": "Habanero pepper (for scotch bonnet)",
                    "ratio": "1:1",
                    "note": "Nearly identical heat level and a similar fruity flavor, though scotch bonnet has a slightly sweeter, less sharp finish.",
                    "ratio_multiplier": 1.0,
                },
                {
                    "name": "Serrano or jalapeno plus a pinch of allspice (for scotch bonnet, lower heat)",
                    "ratio": "2:1",
                    "note": "Use about double the amount of milder chile to approximate the heat, and add a little allspice to recover some of the flavor scotch bonnet contributes.",
                    "ratio_multiplier": 2.0,
                },
                {
                    "name": "Cilantro (for culantro)",
                    "ratio": "1:2",
                    "note": "Culantro is far more concentrated; use roughly twice the amount of cilantro to match the flavor punch of a small amount of culantro.",
                    "ratio_multiplier": 2.0,
                },
                {
                    "name": "Canned coconut milk (for fresh grated coconut milk)",
                    "ratio": "1:1",
                    "note": "Full-fat canned coconut milk works as a direct swap in stews and rice dishes; shake the can well since it separates.",
                    "ratio_multiplier": 1.0,
                },
                {
                    "name": "Green cabbage or spinach (for callaloo greens)",
                    "ratio": "1:1",
                    "note": "Neither matches callaloo's slightly slippery texture exactly, but both work in a pinch for the stewed greens side dish; add a splash of coconut milk to compensate for lost richness.",
                    "ratio_multiplier": 1.0,
                },
                {
                    "name": "Curry powder blend with extra turmeric and fenugreek (for Caribbean-style curry powder)",
                    "ratio": "1:1",
                    "note": "Trinidadian curry powder is toasted and more turmeric-forward than standard Indian curry powder; boost turmeric and add a pinch of fenugreek to get closer to the real flavor.",
                    "ratio_multiplier": 1.0,
                },
            ],
            "storage": "Keep dried allspice berries, whole scotch bonnets, and curry powder in airtight containers away from light, where they hold potency for 6 to 12 months. Fresh scotch bonnets and culantro keep about a week in the refrigerator crisper wrapped loosely in a paper towel. Green seasoning (a blended paste of herbs, garlic, and peppers used as a marinade base) freezes well in ice cube trays for up to 3 months and thaws in minutes when needed for a quick marinade.",
            "uses": "Green seasoning and jerk marinade go on meat and fish hours or overnight before cooking, since the acid and salt need time to penetrate. Scotch bonnet is usually added whole and left uncut in stews and soups for heat and aroma without the risk of the dish becoming unbearably hot, then removed before serving. Coconut milk goes into rice and peas, curries, and stewed fish near the end of cooking so it doesn't split. Plantains are cooked according to ripeness: green ones are fried as savory tostones or boiled, while black, soft ones are pan-fried into sweet maduros.",
            "nutrition_note": "Caribbean staples like rice and peas, plantain, and cassava are calorie-dense carbohydrate sources, while stews built on beans, pigeon peas, and fish add plentiful protein and fiber. Coconut milk contributes saturated fat, so lighter versions of stews often cut it with broth or water.",
            "buying_tips": "Caribbean and West Indian grocery stores carry fresh scotch bonnets, culantro, and green seasoning pastes that are hard to find at general supermarkets. Scotch bonnet peppers should be firm and glossy without soft spots; a wrinkled pepper has lost much of its heat and aroma. For coconut milk, choose cans without added gums if a recipe needs it to reduce and thicken naturally.",
            "pairing_suggestions": "Rice and peas, fried plantain, and a simple cucumber or avocado salad round out heavier stewed dishes like curry goat or oxtail. A cold ginger beer or sorrel drink cuts through the richness of coconut-based curries and jerk marinades.",
            "variety_notes": "Jamaican cooking leans on jerk seasoning, allspice, and scotch bonnet with a drier heat profile. Trinidadian food layers in Indian influence through curries, roti, and doubles, with a toasted, greener curry powder. Puerto Rican and Dominican cooking use sofrito (a garlic, pepper, and herb base) rather than scotch bonnet-forward heat, leaning milder and more tomato-based. Haitian cuisine uses epis, a similar herb paste, alongside French-influenced techniques like braising.",
            "faqs": [
                {
                    "question": "Is Caribbean food always very spicy?",
                    "answer": "Not uniformly. Jamaican jerk dishes and Trinidadian pepper sauces run hot, but Puerto Rican, Dominican, and Cuban cooking are generally mild, relying on herbs and garlic rather than chile heat.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "What is the difference between jerk seasoning and green seasoning?",
                    "answer": "Jerk seasoning is a dry or wet rub built around allspice, scotch bonnet, and thyme, typically used for grilled or smoked meat. Green seasoning is a blended wet paste of herbs, garlic, and peppers used as an everyday marinade base for stews, fish, and rice dishes across Trinidad and other islands.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Can scotch bonnet peppers be swapped 1:1 with habanero in any recipe?",
                    "answer": "Yes for heat level, since both measure in a similar range on the Scoville scale, but scotch bonnet has a slightly fruitier, sweeter undertone that habanero doesn't fully replicate in dishes where the pepper flavor itself matters, like a raw pepper sauce.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Why do some Caribbean stews call for browning sugar before adding meat?",
                    "answer": "This is the browning technique used in Jamaican cooking, where sugar is melted until dark and almost bitter in oil before meat is seared in it, giving stews like brown stew chicken their deep color and slightly caramelized flavor.",
                },
            ],
            "substitute_page_slug": None,
            "recipe_slugs": [],
            "related_ingredient_slugs": [],
        },
    },
