    {
        "slug": "how-to-smoke-a-brisket",
        "template_type": "howto_technique",
        "title": "How to Smoke a Brisket",
        "batch_number": 2,
        "content": {
            "meta_description": "How to smoke a brisket low and slow with the right trim, rub, wrap timing, and rest so it comes out tender and juicy instead of dry or tough.",
            "hero_image_query": "smoked brisket sliced with bark and smoke ring",
            "image_alt": "A whole smoked brisket sliced on a cutting board, showing a dark peppery bark, a pink smoke ring, and moist pulled-apart texture between slices.",
            "intro": "Brisket is a tough, well-worked cut that only becomes tender through hours of low heat breaking down collagen, rushing it or trimming it poorly leads to dry slices or a rubbery, unrendered layer of fat.",
            "steps": [
                "Trim the brisket, leaving about 1/4 inch of fat cap on top and removing any thick, hard fat deposits between the point and flat.",
                "Season generously with a simple rub of coarse salt and coarse black pepper, or a rub with paprika and garlic powder added, covering all sides.",
                "Let the seasoned brisket rest uncovered in the fridge for at least 1 hour, or overnight, so the rub adheres and the surface dries slightly.",
                "Set up the smoker for indirect heat at 225-250°F (107-121°C) using oak or hickory for a steady, clean smoke.",
                "Place the brisket fat-side up (or down, depending on the smoker's heat source location) on the grate, fat cap facing the direct heat source if there is one.",
                "Smoke undisturbed for 6-8 hours, until the internal temperature reaches 160-170°F (71-77°C) and the bark is dark and set.",
                "Wrap tightly in butcher paper or heavy-duty foil once the bark has formed, then return it to the smoker.",
                "Continue smoking until the internal temperature reaches 200-204°F (93-96°C) and a probe slides into the thickest part of the flat with almost no resistance, usually another 3-5 hours.",
                "Rest the wrapped brisket in a dry cooler or an oven set to its lowest temperature for at least 1 hour, and up to 4 hours, before slicing.",
                "Slice the flat against the grain into pencil-thick slices, and slice the point separately since its grain runs a different direction.",
            ],
            "common_mistakes": [
                "Slicing too soon: cutting into a brisket right off the smoker lets all the juice run out onto the board instead of staying in the meat.",
                "Trimming the fat cap too thin: less than 1/4 inch and the surface dries out and burns before the interior finishes rendering.",
                "Chasing time instead of temperature: brisket doneness depends on how easily a probe slides in, not a fixed number of hours, since size and airflow vary.",
                "Wrapping too early: wrapping before the bark has set traps steam and turns the crust soft and pale instead of dark and firm.",
                "Not separating the point from the flat before slicing: the two muscles have different grain directions, so slicing straight through both gives some pieces that are impossible to chew.",
            ],
            "equipment": [
                "Offset smoker or pellet smoker",
                "Instant-read or leave-in meat probe thermometer",
                "Butcher paper or heavy-duty foil",
                "Sharp slicing knife",
                "Cutting board with a juice groove",
                "Insulated cooler (for resting)",
            ],
            "faqs": [
                {
                    "question": "Should brisket be smoked fat-side up or fat-side down?",
                    "answer": "It depends on where the heat source sits. In offset smokers with heat coming from one end, fat-side down toward the firebox shields the meat; in pellet smokers with even heat from below, fat-side up is more common so the fat bastes the meat as it renders.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Why wrap a brisket in butcher paper instead of foil?",
                    "answer": "Butcher paper lets some moisture escape, which keeps the bark firmer, while foil traps all the steam and produces a softer, wetter crust, sometimes called the Texas crutch.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "What internal temperature should brisket be pulled at?",
                    "answer": "Most briskets are done between 200-204°F (93-96°C), but the real test is probe feel: a probe should slide into the flat like it's going into softened butter, with no resistance.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "How long does a brisket need to rest before slicing?",
                    "answer": "At least 1 hour, though holding it wrapped in a cooler or low oven for 2-4 hours lets the juices redistribute fully and makes for noticeably moister slices.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Why is my brisket tough even though it hit 203°F?",
                    "answer": "Collagen breakdown depends on time at temperature, not just the number on the thermometer. A brisket pulled the moment it hits 203°F without enough total cook time can still be underdone; it needs to feel probe-tender, not just reach a target degree.",
                },
            ],
            "recipe_slugs": [],
            "related_technique_slugs": [],
        },
    },
