    {
        "slug": "what-is-evoo",
        "template_type": "definition",
        "title": "What Is EVOO?",
        "batch_number": 4,
        "content": {
            "meta_description": "EVOO stands for extra virgin olive oil, the highest, least processed grade of olive oil. What it is, how it differs from regular olive oil, and how to use it.",
            "hero_image_query": "extra virgin olive oil bottle pouring into dish",
            "hero_image_must_match": ["olive oil", "extra virgin olive oil"],
            "image_alt": "A glass bottle of golden-green extra virgin olive oil being poured over a shallow dish of bread and herbs.",
            "direct_answer": "EVOO is shorthand for extra virgin olive oil, the highest quality grade of olive oil, made by mechanically pressing olives without heat or chemical processing.",
            "expanded_explanation": "Extra virgin olive oil comes from the first cold pressing of olives, meaning no heat or solvents are used to extract the oil, which preserves more of the fruit's natural flavor, aroma, and nutrients. To be legally labeled extra virgin, the oil also has to meet strict standards for acidity (below 0.8%) and pass a taste test for defects like rancidity or mustiness. This is what separates it from regular 'olive oil' or 'light olive oil,' which are often blends of refined oil (processed with heat or chemicals to strip out flaws) and a small amount of virgin oil added back for flavor. EVOO tends to be more peppery, grassy, or fruity depending on the olive variety and where it was grown.",
            "usage_origin": "EVOO is a staple of Mediterranean cooking, used for drizzling over finished dishes, dressing salads, dipping bread, and finishing soups, where its flavor stays front and center. It's also used for lower-heat sautéing, though its stronger flavor and lower smoke point (around 375-410°F) make it less ideal for high-heat frying than refined oils.",
            "substitute_note": "Regular or 'light' olive oil is the closest substitute for high-heat cooking where EVOO's flavor isn't needed, but for finishing dishes, there's no real substitute for EVOO's flavor; a good avocado oil is the nearest neutral-ish stand-in.",
            "link_terms": [],
            "faqs": [
                {
                    "question": "Is EVOO healthier than regular olive oil?",
                    "answer": "EVOO retains more polyphenols and antioxidants than refined olive oil because it isn't processed with heat or chemicals, which strip out some of those compounds along with flavor.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Can you cook with EVOO or is it only for drizzling?",
                    "answer": "You can cook with it at low to medium heat, such as sautéing vegetables, but its lower smoke point and stronger flavor make it less suited to deep frying or high-heat searing.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "How can you tell if EVOO is real or fake?",
                    "answer": "Check for a harvest date and country of origin on the label, and look for third-party certifications like the California Olive Oil Council seal; oils labeled only 'product of Italy' with no harvest date are often blends of lower-quality oils.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Does EVOO go bad?",
                    "answer": "Yes, it degrades with exposure to light, heat, and air, losing flavor and nutritional value over 18-24 months even unopened. Store it in a dark bottle in a cool cabinet, not next to the stove.",
                },
            ],
            "substitute_page_slug": None,
            "related_recipe_slugs": [],
        },
    },
