    {
        "slug": "how-to-tell-if-a-mango-is-ripe",
        "template_type": "howto_technique",
        "title": "How to Tell If a Mango Is Ripe",
        "batch_number": 4,
        "content": {
            "meta_description": "How to tell if a mango is ripe using touch and smell instead of color, plus how to ripen a hard mango faster at home.",
            "hero_image_query": "ripe mangoes on wooden table",
            "image_alt": "Several whole mangoes in varying shades of red, yellow, and green arranged on a wood cutting board, one cut in half showing deep orange flesh.",
            "intro": "Color is the least reliable way to judge a ripe mango since different varieties stay green, red, or yellow even at full ripeness. Touch and smell tell the real story.",
            "steps": [
                "Pick up the mango and gently squeeze it in your palm, the way you would test an avocado.",
                "Feel for a slight give across the whole fruit, not just at one spot. A ripe mango yields slightly under gentle pressure but doesn't feel mushy or leave a dent.",
                "Bring the stem end close to your nose and inhale. A ripe mango smells sweet, slightly floral, and a little like pine or resin.",
                "Check the skin for tiny wrinkles near the stem end, a sign the fruit has started to soften and lose moisture in a normal, ripe way.",
                "Ignore overall skin color and instead look at the base color under any red blush: it should have shifted from tight, hard green to a softer yellow, gold, or orange, depending on the variety.",
                "If the mango is still firm and smells like nothing, let it sit at room temperature for 2-4 days, checking daily, until it gives slightly and smells fragrant at the stem.",
            ],
            "common_mistakes": [
                "Judging ripeness by red skin color: many mango varieties develop a red blush while still rock-hard and underripe, and some ripen fully without ever turning red.",
                "Squeezing too hard: firm pressure can bruise the flesh right under the skin even on a mango that isn't ready yet.",
                "Refrigerating an unripe mango: cold air stops the ripening process almost completely, so a hard mango can stay hard for weeks in the fridge.",
                "Smelling the middle of the fruit instead of the stem end: the stem end is where ripening sugars concentrate and where the aroma is strongest.",
            ],
            "equipment": [
                "Paring knife",
                "Cutting board",
            ],
            "faqs": [
                {
                    "question": "Does a wrinkled mango mean it's overripe?",
                    "answer": "Not necessarily. Light wrinkling near the stem is normal for a ripe mango. Deep wrinkling across the whole skin combined with a sour, fermented smell or leaking juice means it's overripe.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "How can I ripen a mango faster?",
                    "answer": "Place it in a paper bag at room temperature, alone or with a banana or apple. The ethylene gas from the other fruit speeds ripening, usually cutting the wait to 1-2 days.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Why is my mango hard even though it's turning yellow?",
                    "answer": "Some varieties, like Ataulfo, turn yellow before they're fully soft. Rely on touch and smell rather than color alone to confirm ripeness.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Can I eat a mango that's slightly underripe?",
                    "answer": "Yes, it will taste more tart and less sweet, with firmer flesh that's easier to dice cleanly, which is why underripe mango is often used in salads and slaws.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Should a ripe mango be stored in the fridge?",
                    "answer": "Once ripe, refrigerate it and use within 3-5 days to slow further softening without stopping ripeness altogether.",
                },
            ],
            "recipe_slugs": [],
            "related_technique_slugs": [],
        },
    },
