    {
        "slug": "indian-flatbread",
        "template_type": "ingredient_hub",
        "title": "Indian Flatbread",
        "batch_number": 4,
        "content": {
            "meta_description": "What Indian flatbread is, the differences between roti, naan, paratha, and puri, the best substitutes, and how to store and reheat each type.",
            "hero_image_query": "assorted indian flatbreads roti naan paratha on wooden board",
            "image_alt": "A stack of chapati, a charred naan, and a flaky paratha arranged on a dark wooden board with a small bowl of ghee alongside.",
            "description": "Indian flatbread is a broad category of unleavened or lightly leavened breads made mostly from wheat flour, cooked on a tawa (flat griddle), in a tandoor, or fried. The main types differ in flour, fat content, and cooking method: roti and chapati are thin and made from whole wheat atta cooked dry on a griddle, paratha is layered with ghee or oil and pan-fried, naan is leavened with yogurt or yeast and traditionally baked in a tandoor, and puri is small, unleavened dough deep-fried until puffed. All of them function the same way at the table, as an edible utensil for scooping curries, dals, and vegetables.",
            "substitutes": [
                {
                    "name": "Store-bought naan or pita bread",
                    "ratio": "1:1",
                    "note": "Closest texture match for scooping curries; pita is thinner and less chewy than naan but works in a pinch.",
                    "ratio_multiplier": 1.0,
                },
                {
                    "name": "Flour tortillas",
                    "ratio": "1:1",
                    "note": "Similar thinness to roti and easy to find, though they lack the wheaty flavor of atta and won't puff the same way over a flame.",
                    "ratio_multiplier": 1.0,
                },
                {
                    "name": "Lavash",
                    "ratio": "1:1",
                    "note": "Very thin and pliable, good for wrapping but too crisp for scooping thick dals once it cools.",
                    "ratio_multiplier": 1.0,
                },
                {
                    "name": "Basmati or jasmine rice",
                    "ratio": "1 cup cooked rice per 2 flatbreads",
                    "note": "Not a bread substitute in texture, but serves the same functional role of soaking up sauce when flatbread isn't available.",
                    "ratio_multiplier": None,
                },
                {
                    "name": "Homemade roti from all-purpose flour",
                    "ratio": "1:1",
                    "note": "Whole wheat atta gives the real flavor, but a 50/50 blend of all-purpose and whole wheat flour makes a workable dough if atta isn't stocked locally.",
                    "ratio_multiplier": 1.0,
                },
            ],
            "storage": "Wrap cooked flatbreads in a clean kitchen towel while still warm to trap steam and keep them soft; once cool, stack with parchment between layers and store in an airtight bag at room temperature for a day or in the refrigerator for up to 4 days. Freeze with parchment between each piece for up to 2 months.",
            "uses": "Use roti and chapati as the everyday bread for dal, sabzi, and curries. Reserve naan for richer dishes like butter chicken or paneer makhani where its buttery, chewy texture holds up to heavier sauces. Paratha, especially stuffed versions with potato or paneer, works as a standalone meal with yogurt or pickle. Puri is typically served with a lighter, less saucy side like chana masala or aloo sabzi since it's meant to stay crisp.",
            "nutrition_note": "Whole wheat roti is the leanest option, lower in fat than naan or paratha since it's cooked with little to no added oil. Naan and paratha pick up significant fat from ghee, oil, or yogurt in the dough, and puri absorbs oil during deep-frying, roughly doubling the calorie count compared to plain roti of the same size.",
            "buying_tips": "For packaged naan or roti, check the ingredient list for shortening or hydrogenated oil, which shows up in mass-produced versions and changes both flavor and texture. Fresh flatbread from an Indian grocery or restaurant tandoor section is almost always softer and more pliable than shelf-stable packaged versions. If buying atta flour to make your own, look for finely milled whole wheat labeled specifically for chapati or roti rather than generic whole wheat flour, which is coarser and yields a tougher dough.",
            "pairing_suggestions": "Roti and naan pair with dal makhani, saag paneer, and any curry with a moderate amount of sauce. Paratha pairs well with plain yogurt, mango pickle, or a fried egg for breakfast. Puri is traditional alongside bhaji or chana masala for a festive or weekend breakfast spread.",
            "variety_notes": "Roti and chapati are essentially the same thin, dry-griddled bread, with naming varying by region. Naan is distinct in that it uses a leavened, yogurt- or yeast-enriched dough and is traditionally cooked in a tandoor, giving it char spots and a chewier bite. Paratha is layered with fat and pan-fried, which produces a flaky texture closer to a laminated pastry than a simple flatbread. Puri is deep-fried rather than griddled or baked, which puffs it into a hollow ball.",
            "faqs": [
                {
                    "question": "What's the difference between roti and naan?",
                    "answer": "Roti is unleavened, made only from whole wheat flour and water, and cooked dry on a griddle. Naan uses a leavened dough with yogurt or yeast, is enriched with fat, and is traditionally baked in a tandoor, giving it a softer, chewier texture and browned char spots.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Can Indian flatbread be made without a tandoor?",
                    "answer": "Yes. Naan can be cooked on a very hot cast iron skillet or under a broiler; it won't get the exact same smoky char as a clay tandoor, but the dough and flavor are the same.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Why does my roti not puff up on the griddle?",
                    "answer": "Puffing depends on even rolling thickness and enough direct heat to create steam quickly inside the dough. Uneven rolling or a griddle that isn't hot enough before the roti goes on are the two most common causes of a flat roti.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Is naan the same as pita bread?",
                    "answer": "No. Both are leavened flatbreads, but naan dough typically includes yogurt or milk and sometimes egg, giving it a richer, chewier crumb, while pita is a leaner yeast dough that puffs into a pocket during baking.",
                },
            ],
            "substitute_page_slug": None,
            "recipe_slugs": [],
            "related_ingredient_slugs": [],
        },
    },
